# What the quoted-printable encoder does at the start of a line, which some
# mail systems change: a "From " (mbox delivery writes ">From ") has its 'F'
# encoded, also when a soft line break moves it there, and a "Fromage" and
# a "from " (left alone by mbox) do not.  A line of a single '.' (which
# would end the DATA) and a line starting with '.' go out as they are, and
# dot-stuffing takes care of them.
mode=accept
tls=none
form=(From=alice@example.com To=bob@example.net Subject=Starts User=alice Password=secret
      Body="From here
$(printf 'x%.0s' $(seq 75))From there
Fromage
from below
.
.dot")
